[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## What the C program actually does
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m int main() { float x = 0.f; scanf("%f", &x); driver(x); return 0; }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m There is no branching in the C file itself: `print_hex` always writes exactly
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `sizeof(float)` == 4 bytes as `%02x` plus a `\n`, and `main` always returns 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Every input class therefore lives inside `scanf("%f")`. The observable surface is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exactly:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * stdout: 8 lowercase hex digits + `\n` — the object representation of the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   resulting `float` in host byte order (little-endian x86-64 here)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * stderr: always empty
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * exit status: always 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Consequently **no input can change the exit status or stderr**, and the only way
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m to diverge is to produce a different `float`. The tests still assert all three
