[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours that were checked because a naive translation gets them wrong
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These are not mismatches — the translation already handles each one — but they
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m are the places where this program is easy to get wrong, so they are recorded
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m with the input that pins them down and are each covered by a test.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Input | Correct output | Why it is a trap |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `infin` | `00000000` | glibc's `scanf` commits to `infinity` as soon as a 4th `i` follows `inf`; when the rest does not arrive it reports a *matching failure* and assigns nothing, so `x` stays `0.f`. A translation that calls `strtof`-like logic returns `inf` here. Same for `infi`, `infini`, `infinit`. |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-0x` vs `-0x.` | `00000000` vs `00000080` | `0x` with no hex digits is rejected outright (no assignment, sign lost). Adding a radix point makes the token acceptable to the converter, which converts the leading `0` — so the sign survives and the result is **negative** zero. |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-nan` | `0000c0ff` | The sign bit is applied to the NaN. |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `nan(1)`, `nan(0x7fffff)` | `0000c07f` | glibc's `scanf` path ignores the payload; it does **not** fold it into the significand. A translation that implements the `strtod` payload extension would print a different mantissa. |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `0x1.0000003p0` | `0000803f` | Round-to-nearest, **ties to even**, on the dropped low bits — the result is exactly `1.0`, not the next float up. |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `1e` , `1e+` , `0x1p` | value of `1` / `1` / `0x1` | The exponent marker only participates when at least one digit follows it; otherwise it is not part of the subject sequence. |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `1e400`, `0x1p128` | `0000807f` | Overflow yields `+inf` (`HUGE_VALF`), not a saturated finite value or a panic. |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `0x1p-150` | `00000000` | Underflow below `2^-149` rounds to zero (ties to even on the subnormal boundary), it does not clamp to the smallest subnormal. |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `1e99999999999999999999` | `0000807f` | The exponent does not fit in any integer type; it must be clamped rather than wrapped. Signed-overflow wrapping would flip it to an underflow and print `00000000`. |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `\n\n\n3.25` | `0000503f` | `scanf` skips newlines. A `fgets`/read-a-line translation would see an empty line and print `00000000`. |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `0.` + 200,000 `0`s + `1` | `00000000` | Long tokens must not overflow the significand accumulator into a wrong answer. |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | output encoding | `to_ne_bytes` | The C program dumps raw memory, so the byte order is the host's. `to_be_bytes` reverses every non-palindromic result. |
